# Electromagnetic Waves: The Spectrum That Carries the Modern World

> Light, radio, X-rays, and microwaves are all the same physical thing
> — electric and magnetic fields oscillating together, racing through
> vacuum at the speed of light. This lesson maps the full
> electromagnetic spectrum and what each band does for us.

## 1. What Is an Electromagnetic Wave?

[definition]
An **electromagnetic (EM) wave** is a self-sustaining pair of
oscillating fields — electric and magnetic — perpendicular to each
other and to the direction of travel. Unlike sound, it needs no
medium and travels through vacuum at
$c = 3 \times 10^8$ m/s.
[/definition]

James Clerk Maxwell predicted the wave in the 1860s from his
equations; Heinrich Hertz produced and detected radio waves in 1887.
All EM waves share the basic wave relation:

@math label="eq:wave-relation"
c = f\lambda
@/math

Since $c$ is fixed, higher frequency means shorter wavelength — the
single organizing principle of the spectrum.

## 2. The Electromagnetic Spectrum

Ordered from longest wavelength (lowest frequency, least energy) to
shortest (highest frequency, most energetic):

| Band | Wavelength range | Everyday uses |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Radio | > 1 m | AM/FM broadcast, TV, astronomy |
| Microwave | 1 m – 1 mm | Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, radar, ovens, satellite links |
| Infrared (IR) | 1 mm – 700 nm | Remote controls, thermal imaging, night vision |
| Visible light | 700 nm (red) – 400 nm (violet) | Everything we see |
| Ultraviolet (UV) | 400 nm – 10 nm | Sterilisation, vitamin D, tanning |
| X-rays | 10 nm – 0.01 nm | Medical imaging, airport security, crystallography |
| Gamma rays | < 0.01 nm | Cancer radiotherapy, sterilising food, astronomy |

