[info title="Why the sky is blue and sunsets are red"]
Short wavelengths scatter far more strongly than long ones
(Rayleigh scattering varies as $1/\lambda^4$). Air molecules scatter
blue light across the whole sky, while reds and oranges pass straight
through the thick atmosphere at sunset to paint the horizon.
[/info]

## 3. Photons: The Quantum Side

An EM wave also behaves as a stream of particles — **photons** — each
carrying a packet of energy:

@math label="eq:photon-energy"
E = hf
@/math

where $h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}$ J·s is Planck's constant. This is why
gamma rays and X-rays (high $f$) are **ionising** — their photons carry
enough energy to knock electrons off atoms, damaging DNA — while radio
and microwaves are not.

[warning]
Ionising radiation — UV, X-rays, gamma rays — is why you wear a lead
apron at dental X-rays and sun-screen against UV. Damage is
cumulative; dose limits exist for good reason.
[/warning]

## 4. How Each Band Earns Its Keep

### Radio and microwaves — communication

- **Broadcasting:** AM (amplitude modulation) travels far at night via
  ionosphere reflection; FM (frequency modulation) resists noise,
  giving clearer sound.
- **Radar** (Radio Detection and Ranging) times microwave echoes to
  find aircraft range and speed — the police speed gun uses the
  Doppler shift of reflected microwaves.
- **Microwave ovens** target a water-absorbing frequency (~2.45 GHz);
  the waves twist polar water molecules back and forth, heating food
  from within.
- **5G, Wi-Fi, GPS, satellite TV** all live in the microwave band —
  a modern city carries thousands of simultaneous conversations
  through it.

### Infrared — heat and remote sensing

Every warm object glows in IR. Thermal cameras spot heat leaks in
buildings, fevers at airports, and survivors under rubble. The
**James Webb Space Telescope** observes the universe in infrared,
piercing dust to see the earliest galaxies.

### Visible light — the sliver we sense

Less than one octave of the spectrum, yet it powers photosynthesis,
astronomy's first telescopes, and all human vision.

### Ultraviolet and beyond — probing matter

UV sterilises surgical tools and drinking water; X-rays reveal bones,
baggage, and the atomic structure of crystals (DNA's double helix was
confirmed by X-ray diffraction); gamma rays treat tumours and trace
radioactive tracers in the body.

## 5. The Atmosphere's Windows

Earth's atmosphere absorbs most EM radiation but is transparent in a
few **windows** — the reason astronomy and satellite communication
work at all:

| Window | Band | What gets through |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Optical | Visible + near IR | Ground telescopes |
| Radio | ~1 cm to ~10 m | Radio telescopes, GPS, satellite links |
| (Open space) | IR, UV, X-ray, gamma | Only from satellites — hence Hubble, Chandra, JWST |

[tip]
Next time your phone shows full Wi-Fi signal in a concrete room but
drops Bluetooth in the next, you are watching the spectrum at work:
longer waves diffract around obstacles better; 5 GHz Wi-Fi carries
more data than 2.4 GHz but penetrates walls worse.
[/tip]

[summary]
EM waves are coupled oscillating electric and magnetic fields
travelling at $c = 3 \times 10^8$ m/s in vacuum, linked by
$c = f\lambda$. The spectrum runs from radio through microwave,
infrared, visible, ultraviolet, and X-rays to gamma rays, energy per
photon rising as $E = hf$. Low-frequency bands carry communication
and radar; IR reveals heat; only a narrow visible sliver powers
vision; ionising UV, X-rays, and gamma rays both probe and damage
matter — which is exactly why they must be handled with care.
[/summary]
